course: london
name: London System
color: white
eco: D02 A46 D00 A48 A45 A80
tagline: One setup against everything. Bf4, e3, c3, Nf3, Nbd2, Bd3, and go.
description: The London is the easiest repertoire in chess to learn: you put your pieces on the same squares in almost every game. This course teaches the setup, the key plans (Ne5 with f4, or the e4 break), and the exact moves you need against Black's sharpest tries: ...c5 with ...Qb6, early ...Bf5 and ...Bg4, the ...Nh5 bishop hunt, ...Ne4, ...cxd4 structures, King's Indian and Old Indian setups, and offbeat replies like 2...Nc6 and 1...b5.

chapter: classical
title: The main setup vs ...d5
intro: Against 1...d5 and ...e6 you build the London pyramid: pawns on c3, d4 and e3, bishop on f4, knights on f3 and d2, bishop on d3. When Black offers to trade bishops with ...Bd6, drop back to g3. Then jump into e5 and support the knight with f4.

line: main-b6
name: Main line: ...Bd6 and ...b6
moves: 1.d4 {Claim the centre with the d-pawn.} d5 2.Bf4 {The London bishop: develop it before you play e3, so it doesn't get locked in.} Nf6 3.e3 {Build the pyramid and open the f1 bishop.} e6 4.Nf3 c5 5.c3 {Support d4 so ...cxd4 can always be met by exd4 or cxd4.} Nc6 6.Nbd2 {The knight goes to d2, not c3: c3 is for the pawn, and d2 supports e4 or f3.} Bd6 7.Bg3 {Don't trade on d6. If Black takes on g3, hxg3 opens the h-file for your rook.} O-O 8.Bd3 {Your bishop points at h7.} b6 9.Ne5 {The London outpost. The knight is strong on e5 and supports a kingside attack.} Bb7 10.f4 {Secure the knight with f4: a London with a Stonewall-style kingside attack. Next ideas are Qf3, g4 and O-O or O-O-O.}

line: main-re8
name: Main line: ...Bd6 and ...Re8
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Bf4 Nf6 3.e3 e6 4.Nf3 c5 5.c3 Nc6 6.Nbd2 Bd6 7.Bg3 O-O 8.Bd3 Re8 {Black prepares ...e5.} 9.Ne5 {Occupy e5 before Black can play ...e5 there. If Black challenges with ...Nd7?, Nxc6 and Bxd6 win material: the d6 bishop loses its defender.}

line: main-bxg3
name: ...Bxg3: Black takes the bishop
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Bf4 Nf6 3.e3 e6 4.Nf3 c5 5.c3 Nc6 6.Nbd2 Bd6 7.Bg3 Bxg3 {Black gives up the bishop pair to break your structure.} 8.hxg3 {Recapture toward the centre. The open h-file is great for your rook if you castle queenside.} Qd6 9.Bd3 e5 10.dxe5 Nxe5 11.Nxe5 Qxe5 12.Nf3 {Develop with tempo by hitting the queen. White is comfortable.}

line: bd6-early
name: 4...Bd6 straight away
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Bf4 Nf6 3.e3 e6 4.Nf3 Bd6 {Black offers the trade at once.} 5.Bg3 {Same idea: keep the bishop.} O-O 6.Bd3 c5 7.c3 Nc6 8.Nbd2 b6 9.Ne5 {The main-line position by transposition.} Bb7 10.f4

line: qb6
name: ...c5, ...Nc6, ...Qb6: hitting b2
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Bf4 Nf6 3.e3 c5 4.c3 Nc6 5.Nd2 {Move order matters: Nd2 before Nf3 lets your queen reach b3.} Qb6 {Black attacks b2.} 6.Qb3 {Offer the trade. If Black takes, axb3 gives your rook the a-file.} c4 7.Qc2 {The queen still guards b2 along the rank.} e6 8.Ngf3 Be7 9.Be2 O-O 10.O-O {Next is b3 or e4 to break Black's queenside clamp.}

line: qb6-bf5-trap
name: ...Bf5?: the bishop hangs
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Bf4 Nf6 3.e3 c5 4.c3 Qb6 5.Qb3 {Same answer: meet ...Qb6 with Qb3.} c4 6.Qc2 Bf5 {It looks like it gains a tempo on your queen, but d3 and e4 are empty.} 7.Qxf5 {Take the bishop. A whole piece up.}

line: early-c5
name: 2...c5: early counterattack
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Bf4 c5 {Black hits d4 before playing ...Nf6.} 3.e3 Nc6 4.c3 {Hold the centre.} Qb6 5.Qb3 c4 6.Qc2 e6 7.Nd2 Nf6 8.Ngf3 {Same structure as the ...Qb6 line.}

line: bf5
name: ...Bf5: trade off Black's good bishop
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Bf4 Nf6 3.e3 Bf5 {Black copies you.} 4.Bd3 {Offer the trade of light-squared bishops.} Bxd3 5.Qxd3 {Your queen is active and you're ahead in development.} e6 6.Nf3 c5 7.c3 Nc6 8.Nbd2 Be7 9.O-O O-O 10.Ne5 {The familiar outpost.}

line: bg4
name: ...Bg4: the queen is attacked
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Bf4 Nf6 3.e3 Bg4 {Careful: the bishop now hits your queen on d1 through the empty e2 square.} 4.Be2 {Block, and offer the trade.} Bxe2 5.Qxe2 e6 6.Nf3 c5 7.c3 Nc6 8.Nbd2 Bd6 9.Bg3 O-O 10.O-O {A comfortable London.}

line: c6-bf5
name: ...c6 and ...Bf5: the Slav setup
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Bf4 Nf6 3.e3 c6 {Black prepares ...Bf5 with ...c6 support.} 4.Nf3 Bf5 5.Bd3 {Offer the trade.} Bxd3 6.Qxd3 e6 7.Nbd2 Nbd7 8.O-O Be7 9.e4 {The central break: you're better developed.}

line: e6-first
name: 1...e6 move order
moves: 1.d4 e6 2.Bf4 c5 3.e3 Nc6 4.c3 d5 5.Nd2 Nf6 6.Ngf3 Bd6 7.Bg3 O-O 8.Bd3 {The usual London position.}

line: qb6-after-nf3
name: ...Qb6 after Nf3
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Bf4 Nf6 3.e3 e6 4.Nf3 c5 5.c3 Qb6 {Hit b2 with the knight already on f3.} 6.Qb3 {Same recipe.} c4 7.Qc2 Nc6 8.Nbd2 {Next b3 or e4 to break the clamp.}

chapter: indian
title: Against 1...Nf6 and King's Indian setups
intro: After 1...Nf6 you keep the same setup. Against ...g6 and ...Bg7 put your bishop on e2 and play h3, so the f4 bishop can retreat to h2 and survive ...Nh5 and ...e5. Against an early ...c5 with ...Qb6, know your one sharp answer.

line: kid
name: King's Indian setup with ...e5
moves: 1.d4 Nf6 2.Bf4 {Same London move.} g6 3.e3 Bg7 4.Nf3 O-O 5.Be2 {Against the fianchetto the bishop is better on e2: it doesn't run into ...e5-e4 forks.} d6 6.h3 {Give the bishop a retreat on h2 and stop ...Nh5 and ...Bg4.} c5 7.c3 Nc6 8.O-O Qb6 {Pressure on b2.} 9.Qb3 {The standard answer: offer the queen trade.} Be6 10.Qxb6 axb6 11.a3 {Watch the a-file: the a2 pawn was hanging to ...Rxa2. Now Black's doubled b-pawns are a long-term target.}

line: kid-e5
name: ...e5?: grab the pawn
moves: 1.d4 Nf6 2.Bf4 g6 3.e3 Bg7 4.Nf3 O-O 5.Be2 d6 6.h3 c5 7.c3 Nc6 8.O-O e5 {Natural, but it drops a pawn.} 9.dxe5 dxe5 10.Nxe5 {The c6 knight is overloaded: after ...Nxe5 Bxe5 the bishop also hits the g7 bishop.} Nxe5 11.Bxe5 {A clean extra pawn.}

line: grunfeld
name: ...g6 with ...d5
moves: 1.d4 Nf6 2.Bf4 g6 3.e3 Bg7 4.Nf3 O-O 5.Be2 d5 {A Grünfeld-style setup.} 6.O-O c5 7.c3 Nc6 8.Nbd2 {The usual setup. Ne5 and h3 come next.}

line: nf6-e6
name: 1...Nf6 2...e6 transposition
moves: 1.d4 Nf6 2.Bf4 e6 3.e3 c5 4.c3 Nc6 5.Nd2 d5 6.Ngf3 Bd6 7.Bg3 O-O 8.Bd3 {Back in the main line.}

line: c5-qb6
name: 2...c5 3...Qb6: the sharp test
moves: 1.d4 Nf6 2.Bf4 c5 3.e3 Qb6 {Black attacks b2 at once.} 4.Nc3 {Gambit the b-pawn! If Black takes, the queen gets chased around.} Qxb2 5.Nb5 {Threatens Nc7+ forking king and rook.} Na6 6.a3 {Quiet but deadly: Rb1 next will trap the queen, and Black has to give up material to get it out.}

line: c5-cxd4
name: 2...c5 3...cxd4
moves: 1.d4 Nf6 2.Bf4 c5 3.e3 cxd4 4.exd4 {Recapture with the pawn: the e-file opens and your bishop is outside the chain.} d5 5.Nf3 Nc6 6.c3 Bg4 7.Nbd2 e6 8.Qb3 {Hit b7. Black's queenside is the target.}

line: dutch
name: 1...f5: the Dutch
moves: 1.d4 f5 {Black grabs e4.} 2.Bf4 Nf6 3.e3 e6 4.Nf3 b6 5.Bd3 {Aim at the weakened kingside.} Bb7 6.Nbd2 Be7 7.h3 O-O 8.O-O {Solid: c4 or Qe2 and e4 later.}

line: queens-indian
name: 2...b6: Queen's Indian setup
moves: 1.d4 Nf6 2.Bf4 b6 {Black fianchettoes the light-squared bishop.} 3.e3 Bb7 4.Nd2 {The knight supports e4 against the b7 bishop.} e6 5.Ngf3 c5 6.c3 Be7 7.h3 {A retreat square for the f4 bishop.} O-O 8.Bd3

line: leningrad
name: 1...f5 with ...g6: the Leningrad Dutch
moves: 1.d4 f5 2.Bf4 Nf6 3.e3 g6 {The Leningrad setup.} 4.Nf3 Bg7 5.Be2 O-O 6.h3 {Same idea as against the King's Indian.} d6 7.O-O

line: benoni
name: 1...c5: the Benoni
moves: 1.d4 c5 {Black hits d4 at once.} 2.d5 {Take space: this is the best answer.} Nf6 3.Nc3 d6 4.e4 g6 5.Nf3 Bg7 6.Be2 {A classical big centre. Plans: O-O, Nd2 and f4 or a4.}

chapter: deeper
title: Deeper theory: Black's best tries against the setup
intro: Strong players don't just copy the main line. They hunt your bishop with ...Nh5, trade it with ...Ne4, develop their light bishop before ...e6 and then hit b2, or open the c-file with ...cxd4. The setup stays the same; what changes is knowing the one or two precise moves each idea asks for. A recurring theme: once Black's light-squared bishop leaves c8, b7 is loose, and Qb3 hits it.

line: be7
name: 4...Be7: the Queen's Gambit setup
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Bf4 Nf6 3.e3 e6 4.Nf3 Be7 {Solid: the bishop avoids your Bg3 and waits for ...c5.} 5.Bd3 {Aim at h7 straight away.} O-O 6.O-O c5 7.c3 Nc6 8.Nbd2 b6 9.Ne5 {The outpost again.} Nxe5 10.dxe5 {Recapture with the pawn: it gains space and kicks the f6 knight away from the defence of h7.} Nd7 11.Qg4 {Now the queen joins the attack on the kingside, with Bxh7+ ideas in the air.}

line: ne4
name: ...Ne4: trading off the bishop
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Bf4 Nf6 3.e3 e6 4.Nf3 Bd6 5.Bg3 Ne4 {Black jumps in to take on g3.} 6.Nbd2 {Challenge the knight. If it takes on g3, the h-file opens for your rook.} Nxg3 7.hxg3 c5 8.c3 {The usual pyramid, with a half-open h-file as a bonus. Bd3 and Qc2 hit h7 twice.}

line: e6-bd6
name: 2...e6 and 3...Bd6
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Bf4 e6 3.e3 Bd6 {Black offers the bishop trade before ...Nf6.} 4.Bg3 {Same rule: keep the bishop.} Nf6 5.Nf3 {The ...Bd6 line by transposition.}

line: bf5-early
name: 2...Bf5: the copycat move order
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Bf4 Bf5 {Black copies you before ...Nf6.} 3.e3 e6 4.Bd3 {Same recipe: offer the light-squared bishops.} Bxd3 5.Qxd3 Nf6 6.Nf3 {The ...Bf5 line by transposition.}

line: nh5
name: ...Nh5: hunting the bishop
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Bf4 Nf6 3.e3 c5 4.c3 Nc6 5.Nd2 e6 6.Ngf3 Nh5 {The knight hunts your dark-squared bishop.} 7.Bg5 {Step aside with tempo: the bishop now hits the queen on d8.} Be7 8.Bxe7 Qxe7 9.Ne5 {The dark bishops are off, so the e5 square is yours.} Nxe5 10.dxe5 g6 11.g4 {Hit the h5 knight: its only square is g7, far from the action.}

line: bf5-qb3
name: ...Bf5 with ...c5: hitting b7
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Bf4 Nf6 3.e3 c5 4.c3 Nc6 5.Nd2 Bf5 {The bishop gets out before ...e6.} 6.Ngf3 e6 7.Qb3 {With the bishop gone from c8, b7 is loose.} Qc8 8.Nh4 {Chase the bishop and win the bishop pair.} Be4 9.f3 Bg6 10.Nxg6 hxg6 11.g4 {You have the two bishops; now take space on the kingside. Careful: the natural 11.Bd3? runs into ...c4, hitting your queen and bishop at once.}

line: bg4-qb3
name: ...Bg4 with ...c5: the same b7 idea
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Bf4 Nf6 3.e3 c5 4.c3 Nc6 5.Nd2 Bg4 {Now the bishop pins nothing, because your knight is not on f3 yet.} 6.Qb3 {Hit b7 at once.} Qd7 7.h3 {Ask the bishop where it is going.} Bf5 8.Ngf3 c4 9.Qd1 {The queen retreats; b3 and e4 will break Black's queenside clamp later.}

line: cxd4
name: ...cxd4: the exchange structure
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Bf4 Nf6 3.e3 c5 4.c3 Nc6 5.Nd2 cxd4 {Black releases the tension.} 6.exd4 {Take back with the e-pawn: the e-file opens and the f4 bishop stays outside the pawn chain.} Bf5 7.Qb3 {b7 again.} Qc8 8.Ngf3 e6 9.Nh4 {Chase the bishop.} Be4 10.f3 Bg6 11.Nxg6 hxg6 12.Bd3 {The bishop pair in a structure where your pieces are more active.}

line: grunfeld-d5
name: 1...d5 then ...g6
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Bf4 Nf6 3.e3 g6 {Black goes for a Grünfeld-style setup.} 4.Nf3 Bg7 5.Be2 O-O 6.O-O c5 7.c3 Nc6 8.Nbd2 {The same position as the ...g6 with ...d5 line.}

chapter: offbeat
title: Offbeat replies
intro: Some players avoid main lines altogether: the Chigorin-style 2...Nc6, the Old Indian with ...d6 and ...Nbd7, the Modern with 1...g6, or the Polish 1...b5. Each has a simple answer you can learn once.

line: chigorin-bf5
name: 2...Nc6 3...Nf6 4...Bf5
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Bf4 Nc6 {The knight blocks Black's c-pawn but pressures d4 and e5.} 3.e3 Nf6 4.Nf3 Bf5 5.Bb5 {Pin the knight: Ne5 next will hit c6 twice.} e6 6.Ne5 a6 7.Bxc6+ bxc6 8.g4 {Gain space with tempo on the bishop. Black's doubled c-pawns are a long-term target.}

line: chigorin-bg4
name: 2...Nc6 3...Nf6 4...Bg4
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Bf4 Nc6 3.e3 Nf6 4.Nf3 Bg4 5.h3 {Ask the bishop at once.} Bxf3 6.Qxf3 e6 7.c3 {You have the bishop pair; Nd2 and Bd3 come next.}

line: chigorin-nb4
name: 2...Nc6 3...Nb4: the c2 raid
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Bf4 Nc6 3.e3 Nb4 {The knight eyes c2, but your queen guards it.} 4.a3 {Kick the knight.} Nc6 5.c4 {Black has spent two moves going nowhere, so open the centre while you're ahead in development.}

line: old-indian
name: 2...d6 and ...Nbd7: the Old Indian
moves: 1.d4 Nf6 2.Bf4 d6 {Black prepares ...e5 with tempo on your bishop.} 3.e3 Nbd7 4.h3 {Give the bishop a retreat first, so ...Nh5 can't trap or trade it.} e5 5.Bh2 {The bishop stays on the b8-h2 diagonal, eyeing e5 and d6.} g6 6.Nf3 exd4 7.Nxd4 {A centralised knight, and d6 is a backward pawn to aim at.}

line: modern
name: 1...g6: the Modern move order
moves: 1.d4 g6 2.Bf4 Bg7 3.e3 d6 4.Nf3 Nf6 5.Be2 O-O 6.h3 {The King's Indian position by transposition: the bishop can retreat to h2.}

line: polish
name: 1...b5: the Polish
moves: 1.d4 b5 {An offbeat flank move: the b5 pawn is loose.} 2.e4 {Break the London habit here: the big centre is free, and e4 attacks nothing yet but makes Bxb5 possible.} Bb7 3.Bxb5 {Take the pawn. Black can win back e4, but you develop fast.} Bxe4 4.Nf3 e6 5.O-O {Castle: c4 and Nc3 will hit the e4 bishop next, and you're well ahead in development.}

quiz: main-b6 13
q: Black played ...Bd6, offering a bishop trade. What's the London answer?
a: Bg3: keep the bishop; if ...Bxg3 then hxg3 opens your h-file
w: Bxd6: trade and double Black's pawns
w: Ne5: ignore it
why: The f4 bishop is your best minor piece. Dropping back to g3 keeps it. If Black trades, you recapture toward the centre and get a semi-open h-file.

quiz: main-b6 18
q: You've just put a knight on e5. Which pawn move supports it and builds a kingside attack?
a: f4
w: c4
w: h3
why: f2-f4 locks the knight on e5 and gives a Stonewall-style attacking setup with Qf3, g4 and Rf3-h3 ideas.

quiz: qb6 10
q: Black's queen hits b2. What's the standard London answer?
a: Qb3: offer the queen trade
w: b3: defend the pawn
w: Qc2: guard b2 along the rank
why: Qb3 meets the threat head on. If ...Qxb3, axb3 gives you the half-open a-file. Black usually plays ...c4, and your queen drops to c2, still guarding b2. Watch out for ...Bf5 there: with d3 and e4 empty, Qxf5 just wins the bishop.

quiz: kid 12
q: Why play h3 against the King's Indian setup?
a: It makes h2 a retreat square for the bishop and stops ...Nh5 and ...Bg4
w: To prepare g4 and a pawn storm
w: To stop ...Qh4
why: Against ...g6 and ...e5, Black wants to trap or trade your f4 bishop. With h3 the bishop can always drop back to h2.

quiz: nh5 13
q: Black's knight jumps to h5 to hunt your f4 bishop. Where does the bishop go?
a: Bg5: out of reach, and it hits the queen on d8
w: Bg3: keep it on the diagonal
w: Be5: block the e-pawn
why: On g3 the bishop just gets taken. Bg5 dodges the knight with tempo on the queen, and after ...Be7 Bxe7 the dark-squared bishops are off, leaving e5 to your knight and the h5 knight stranded.

quiz: cxd4 13
q: Black's light-squared bishop has left c8. Which move punishes it?
a: Qb3: the b7 pawn is now undefended
w: Qa4+: check first
w: Bd3: trade bishops
why: The c8 bishop was the b7 pawn's only defender. Qb3 hits b7 (and d5 behind it), so Black has to spend a move like ...Qc8 just to hold on.
